The issue: we don't actually know what collection_date represents, and three sources disagree.

The busyness indicator needs a date that reflects when someone traveled, not when they bought or processed a ticket. collection_date was the field proposed as the best available proxy. Before building on it, we checked it three ways, and got three different answers:

1. What we observed empirically in the data itself. collection_date is populated on every single one of the 3.73 billion rows — including e-tickets and mobile tickets, which have no physical "collection" step at all. It's also the field the underlying table is physically organized (partitioned) by, which is usually a sign of a pipeline/loading date rather than a passenger-facing event. Taken alone, this looks more like "when this record entered our systems" than "when someone traveled."

2. A separate LENNON reference document you shared. It describes a field called travel_date — not collection_date — defined as "date when the ticket was collected; the closest date we have to the day the ticket was used." That's a near-perfect match in wording for what we'd want collection_date to mean, but it's a different field name, and travel_date doesn't appear anywhere in the view we've been given under that name.

3. ONS's own published methodology on this exact dataset (their Jan 2022 rail fares paper). Here it gets more confusing rather than less: ONS's own Prices Division states plainly that their internal proxy for "collection date" is a third field entirely — issuing_datetime — with the explicit caveat that for tickets bought and issued in the same transaction (the majority of modern e-tickets), the issue date is identical to the sale date, so it isn't a meaningful travel-date signal for most tickets either.

So: three names, three plausible-sounding definitions, no clean way to reconcile them from the outside. Our best guess is that travel_date may be a newer field added after ONS's 2022 paper was written — specifically to fix the weakness they flagged in using issuing_datetime — and that collection_date in our view is that newer field, just exposed under a different name. But that's an inference chain across three separate documents, not something we can confirm ourselves.

The specific question to ask:

What does the collection_date column in this view actually correspond to in the source LENNON schema — is it the same field described elsewhere as travel_date, is it something else entirely, and is it distinct from issuing_datetime? If there's a recommended "best available proxy for travel date" field, which one should we be using?

This is worth prioritizing above the other data-team questions — it's foundational to the entire first indicator (busyness by travel date), and everything else about that indicator depends on getting this right first.
